{#- The tab container.

    Both loops read `children`, not `body`: by the time this template runs, the
    body is one string of rendered markup and a label cannot be recovered from
    it. `children` carries each `{% tab %}` with its attributes still resolved
    and its own body separate, which is what makes the strip constructible.

    The panels are emitted flush against each other -- no blank line between a
    `</div>` and the next `<div` -- because a blank line would end the raw-HTML
    block and the following panel would be read as markdown text. Each panel's
    body arrives between blank lines of its own, because the `tab` manifest
    declares `body: markdown`; that is what lets a panel's markdown render
    inside its `<div>` while the panels themselves stay one HTML block.

    `tabs.js` handles switching, group synchronisation, keyboard navigation and
    persistence. The first tab is marked active here so the page is correct
    before any script runs.

    `data-canonical` and `data-part` are the markup contract every conforming
    theme's tabs carry: the tab list, each tab and each panel.
-#}
{%- set group = attrs.group | default("default") -%}
<div class="tabs" data-canonical="tabs" data-tab-group="{{ group }}"><div class="tabs-nav" role="tablist" data-part="tablist">
{%- for c in children if c.name == "tab" %}{% set id = c.attrs.id | default(c.attrs.label | slugify) %}<button class="tabs-tab{{ " active" if loop.first else "" }}" role="tab" data-part="tab" aria-selected="{{ "true" if loop.first else "false" }}" data-tab="{{ id }}" aria-controls="tab-panel-{{ id }}">{{ c.attrs.label }}</button>{% endfor -%}
</div>
{%- for c in children if c.name == "tab" %}{% set id = c.attrs.id | default(c.attrs.label | slugify) %}<div class="tabs-panel{{ " active" if loop.first else "" }}" role="tabpanel" data-part="panel" id="tab-panel-{{ id }}">
{{ c.body }}
</div>{% endfor -%}
</div>
